    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d47">

                <pb facs="0154" n="42" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg42"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 835.73/75</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-09T17:00:00-04:16">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 9, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My April 2, 11 a.m. I have just come from an audience with the President, granted
                    on the subject of the petition of the Central &amp; South American Telegraph Co.
                    to lay a cable from Buenos Aires to Montevideo. I explained the matter fully and
                    completely to him: its past history; the objection raised by the British or
                    “Western” company, based on their alleged monopoly; the objections to this claim
                    under the Argentine Constitution; the reasons of convenience for the completion
                    of this, the last link in the chain of the Pan American cable from New York
                    around South America; the international need of such a system, both to Argentina
                    and Uruguay; and, finally, the particular interest the Government of the United
                    States took in the matter.</p>
                <p>He appeared to be much interested, and assured me he would give the matter
                    serious and friendly consideration himself, and asked me to leave with him
                    personally the copy of the brief application with me, prepared by Mr. Shirley,
                    and the map showing the lines of the telegraph company.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
